w a motley but powerful array of self-understood minorities reshaped American democracy as they battled laws regulating Sabbath observance, alcohol, and interracial contact. Proponents justified these measures with the 'democratic' axiom of majority rule. In response, immigrants, Black northerners, abolitionists, liquor dealers, Catholics, Jews, Seventh-day Baptists, and others articulated a different vision of democracy requiring the protection of minority rights.","thumbnailUrl":"/book-covers/book-moral-minorities-and-the-making-of-american-democracy-kyle-g-volk.jpg","displaySummary":"In Moral Minorities and the Making of American Democracy, historian Kyle G. Volk unearths the origins of modern ideas and practices of minority-rights politics. Focusing on controversies spurred by the explosion of grassroots moral reform in the early nineteenth century, he shows how a motley but powerful array of self-understood minorities reshaped American democracy as they battled laws regulating Sabbath observance, alcohol, and interracial contact. Proponents justified these measures with the 'democratic' axiom of majority rule. In response, immigrants, Black northerners, abolitionists, liquor dealers, Catholics, Jews, Seventh-day Baptists, and others articulated a different vision of democracy requiring the protection of minority rights.","primarySubject":"Race & Ethnicity","primaryTopic":"Black History & Culture","subjectEvidence":{"primarySubject":"Race & Ethnicity","confidence":"low","method":"evidence_score","candidates":[{"subject":"Race & Ethnicity","score":4,"evidenceCount":2},{"subject":"American History","score":4,"evidenceCount":2},{"subject":"Politics & Government","score":3,"evidenceCount":2},{"subject":"History","score":2,"evidenceCount":2},{"subject":"Religion","score":1,"evidenceCount":1}],"evidence":[{"source":"award_category","rawLabel":"American History","mappedSubject":"American History","score":2,"confidence":"low","sourceId":"source-award-record-oah-frederic